{"module":"global-freedom","voice":"female","duration":173.3,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"The global Black freedom struggle","start":0.0,"end":2.36,"words":[[0.04,0.21],[0.21,0.79],[0.79,1.12],[1.12,1.61],[1.61,2.2]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Black Americans' fight for freedom has always been part of a worldwide movement.","start":3.16,"end":7.79,"words":[[3.21,3.48],[3.48,4.09],[4.09,4.36],[4.36,4.5],[4.5,4.9],[4.9,5.11],[5.11,5.51],[5.51,5.72],[5.72,5.92],[5.92,6.06],[6.06,6.19],[6.19,6.73],[6.73,7.28]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Ideas, people and solidarity crossed the Atlantic in both directions.","start":8.14,"end":13.51,"words":[[8.19,8.74],[9.01,9.41],[9.41,9.64],[9.64,10.42],[10.42,10.82],[10.82,10.97],[10.97,11.6],[11.6,11.76],[11.76,12.07],[12.07,12.94]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Pan-Africanism","start":13.86,"end":15.21,"words":[[13.91,15.07]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The first Pan-African Conference met in London in 1900; W.E.B. Du Bois, who attended, organized Pan-African Congresses beginning in Paris in 1919.","start":15.91,"end":28.18,"words":[[15.96,16.11],[16.11,16.42],[16.42,17.19],[17.19,17.8],[17.8,18.03],[18.03,18.18],[18.18,18.64],[18.64,18.79],[18.79,19.94],[20.21,21.05],[21.43,21.59],[21.59,21.97],[22.24,22.39],[22.39,23.0],[23.27,23.88],[23.88,24.65],[24.65,25.34],[25.34,25.87],[25.87,26.03],[26.03,26.41],[26.41,26.56],[26.56,27.71]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Marcus Garvey's Universal Negro Improvement Association, founded in Jamaica in 1914 and based in Harlem, became the largest Black mass movement of its time, with chapters around the world.","start":28.53,"end":41.69,"words":[[28.57,28.95],[28.95,29.39],[29.39,30.06],[30.06,30.51],[30.51,31.26],[31.26,32.23],[32.49,33.01],[33.01,33.16],[33.16,33.76],[33.76,33.91],[33.91,34.8],[34.8,35.02],[35.02,35.4],[35.4,35.55],[35.55,35.99],[36.26,36.7],[36.7,36.85],[36.85,37.37],[37.37,37.67],[37.67,37.9],[37.9,38.42],[38.42,38.57],[38.57,38.79],[38.79,39.16],[39.43,39.65],[39.65,40.17],[40.17,40.62],[40.62,40.77],[40.77,41.14]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In the 1930s, writers Aimé Césaire and Léopold Sédar Senghor launched the Négritude movement, celebrating African identity against French colonial culture.","start":42.04,"end":53.03,"words":[[42.09,42.23],[42.23,42.38],[42.38,43.31],[43.57,44.0],[44.0,44.36],[44.36,44.79],[44.79,45.01],[45.01,45.66],[45.66,46.02],[46.02,46.38],[46.38,46.81],[46.81,46.95],[46.95,47.68],[47.68,48.25],[48.5,49.3],[49.3,49.8],[49.8,50.45],[50.45,50.88],[50.88,51.32],[51.32,52.04],[52.04,52.54]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Independence","start":53.93,"end":55.08,"words":[[53.99,54.94]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Ghana became one of the first sub-Saharan African colonies to win independence, and the first in West Africa, in 1957, led by Kwame Nkrumah; Dr. King attended the celebrations.","start":55.78,"end":69.72,"words":[[55.83,56.14],[56.14,56.6],[56.6,56.83],[56.83,56.98],[56.98,57.14],[57.14,57.44],[57.44,58.21],[58.21,58.75],[58.75,59.29],[59.29,59.44],[59.44,59.67],[59.67,60.59],[60.86,61.09],[61.09,61.24],[61.24,61.55],[61.55,61.71],[61.71,62.01],[62.01,62.55],[62.82,62.97],[62.97,64.36],[64.62,64.85],[64.85,65.08],[65.08,65.47],[65.47,66.08],[66.35,66.81],[67.2,67.43],[67.43,67.96],[67.96,68.12],[68.12,69.12]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Dozens of African nations won independence in the following decade, and their leaders and diplomats at the United Nations pressed the U.S. on segregation during the Cold War.","start":70.07,"end":79.85,"words":[[70.12,70.5],[70.5,70.63],[70.63,71.07],[71.07,71.51],[71.51,71.7],[71.7,72.39],[72.39,72.52],[72.52,72.64],[72.64,73.08],[73.08,73.52],[73.74,73.93],[73.93,74.12],[74.12,74.44],[74.44,74.63],[74.63,75.19],[75.19,75.32],[75.32,75.44],[75.44,75.95],[75.95,76.39],[76.39,76.7],[76.7,76.83],[76.83,77.14],[77.46,77.59],[77.59,78.28],[78.28,78.72],[78.72,78.84],[78.84,79.16],[79.16,79.35]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Taking America's racism to the world","start":80.75,"end":83.19,"words":[[80.8,81.26],[81.26,81.87],[81.87,82.48],[82.48,82.64],[82.64,82.79],[82.79,83.1]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 1951 the Civil Rights Congress, led by William Patterson and Paul Robeson, petitioned the United Nations with We Charge Genocide, documenting lynchings and police killings of Black Americans.","start":83.89,"end":97.76,"words":[[83.94,84.09],[84.09,85.23],[85.23,85.38],[85.38,85.76],[85.76,86.14],[86.14,86.75],[87.02,87.24],[87.24,87.47],[87.47,87.93],[87.93,88.46],[88.46,88.69],[88.69,88.92],[88.92,89.52],[89.79,90.4],[90.4,90.55],[90.55,91.16],[91.16,91.69],[91.69,91.92],[91.92,92.07],[92.07,92.45],[92.45,93.21],[93.47,94.31],[94.31,94.92],[94.92,95.15],[95.15,95.53],[95.53,95.98],[95.98,96.13],[96.13,96.44],[96.44,97.2]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 1964 Malcolm X founded the Organization of Afro-American Unity and sought to bring the U.S. before the UN over human rights.","start":98.11,"end":106.54,"words":[[98.15,98.29],[98.29,99.3],[99.3,99.7],[99.7,99.9],[99.9,100.37],[100.37,100.51],[100.51,101.31],[101.31,101.45],[101.45,102.32],[102.32,102.73],[102.73,102.93],[102.93,103.13],[103.13,103.26],[103.26,103.53],[103.53,103.67],[103.67,104.0],[104.34,104.61],[104.61,104.74],[104.74,105.01],[105.01,105.28],[105.28,105.68],[105.68,106.09]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The U.S. government's concern about its image abroad helped push civil rights reforms, historians such as Mary Dudziak have shown.","start":106.89,"end":114.69,"words":[[106.94,107.07],[107.07,107.4],[107.73,108.38],[108.38,108.78],[108.78,109.11],[109.11,109.3],[109.3,109.63],[109.63,109.96],[109.96,110.29],[110.29,110.48],[110.48,110.81],[110.81,111.14],[111.14,111.6],[111.83,112.48],[112.48,112.75],[112.75,112.88],[112.88,113.21],[113.21,113.73],[113.73,113.93],[113.93,114.26]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Against apartheid","start":115.59,"end":116.89,"words":[[115.64,116.15],[116.15,116.74]]},{"el":"s3","text":"American students, churches, unions and the Congressional Black Caucus built a divestment movement against South African apartheid in the 1970s and 1980s, leading to sanctions passed over a presidential veto in 1986 (see “A world system”).","start":117.59,"end":135.8,"words":[[117.65,118.28],[118.28,119.06],[119.34,119.96],[120.24,120.79],[120.79,121.02],[121.02,121.18],[121.18,122.04],[122.04,122.35],[122.35,122.74],[122.74,123.06],[123.06,123.21],[123.21,124.07],[124.07,124.62],[124.62,125.09],[125.09,125.4],[125.4,125.95],[125.95,126.5],[126.5,126.66],[126.66,126.81],[126.81,127.99],[127.99,128.22],[128.22,129.24],[129.52,129.91],[129.91,130.06],[130.06,130.69],[130.69,131.0],[131.0,131.32],[131.32,131.47],[131.47,132.33],[132.33,132.73],[132.73,132.88],[132.88,134.06],[134.06,134.21],[134.21,134.37],[134.37,134.68],[134.68,135.23]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Nelson Mandela thanked them when he visited the U.S. in 1990.","start":136.15,"end":140.25,"words":[[136.2,136.56],[136.56,136.98],[136.98,137.28],[137.28,137.46],[137.46,137.64],[137.64,137.76],[137.76,138.18],[138.18,138.3],[138.3,138.6],[138.9,139.02],[139.02,139.86]]},{"el":"h4","text":"2020 and after","start":141.15,"end":142.76,"words":[[141.17,142.1],[142.1,142.33],[142.33,142.64]]},{"el":"s4","text":"After George Floyd's murder, protests spread to cities around the world.","start":143.46,"end":148.36,"words":[[143.51,143.81],[143.81,144.18],[144.18,144.63],[144.63,145.01],[145.27,145.94],[145.94,146.32],[146.32,146.47],[146.47,146.84],[146.84,147.29],[147.29,147.44],[147.44,147.81]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In Bristol, England, protesters toppled the statue of slave trader Edward Colston into the harbor.","start":148.71,"end":155.58,"words":[[148.76,148.91],[148.91,149.51],[149.77,150.36],[150.63,151.3],[151.3,151.75],[151.75,151.9],[151.9,152.34],[152.34,152.49],[152.49,152.87],[152.87,153.31],[153.31,153.69],[153.69,154.21],[154.21,154.51],[154.51,154.66],[154.66,155.03]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The United Nations declared an International Decade for People of African Descent (2015–2024), held the first session of its Permanent Forum on People of African Descent in 2022, and proclaimed a second decade in 2024.","start":155.93,"end":172.44,"words":[[155.98,156.13],[156.13,156.72],[156.72,157.24],[157.24,157.76],[157.76,157.9],[157.9,158.72],[158.72,159.16],[159.16,159.31],[159.31,159.68],[159.68,159.83],[159.83,160.34],[160.34,160.79],[160.79,162.93],[163.19,163.48],[163.48,163.63],[163.63,163.93],[163.93,164.3],[164.3,164.44],[164.44,164.67],[164.67,165.26],[165.26,165.63],[165.63,165.77],[165.77,166.14],[166.14,166.29],[166.29,166.81],[166.81,167.25],[167.25,167.4],[167.4,168.51],[168.77,168.99],[168.99,169.65],[169.65,169.8],[169.8,170.25],[170.25,170.69],[170.69,170.84],[170.84,171.95]]}]}